          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatization
            of differentiation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            was introduced in
          %ul
            %li#Kock77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A simple axiomatics for differentiation
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/24491223"} JSTOR
          %p
            Another
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
            formalization of the chain rule in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            is given in
          %ul
            %li#Wellen18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Wellen"}> Felix Wellen
              , section 3 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/thesis+Wellen"} Cartan Geometry in Modal Homotopy Type Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.05966"}> arXiv:1806.05966
              )
          %p Discussion of differential forms as functions on infinitesimal simplices is in
          %ul
            %li#Stel13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Herman+Stel"}> Herman Stel
              ,
              %em
                Cosimplicial
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
                rings and the de Rham complex of Euclidean space
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.7407"}> arXiv:1310.7407
              )
          %h3#history History
          %p
            Discussion of the history of the concept of differentiation, with emphasis on its roots all the way back in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zeno%27s+paradoxes+of+motion"} Zeno's paradoxes of motion
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Boyer49
              Carl Benjamin Boyer,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The history of the Calculus and its conceptual development
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Without the continuity assumption on derivatives, the class of (once-)differentiable functions exhibits non-trivial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descriptive+set+theory"} descriptive set-theoretic
                  complexity, as may be surmised by
                  %a{:href => "http://journals.cambridge.org/action/displayAbstract?fromPage=online&aid=6984080"} this paper
                  by Kechris and Woodin. Thus the continuity assumption eliminates troublesome pathologies.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  According to John Bell, the fact that classically this is merely a point bears comparison to Bishop Berkeley’s objections to the differential calculus he put forth in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em The Analyst
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
